From 20c0692327aa42fd176cf0a184c37b1ba5e3f3ae Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 00:10:06 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] libfs: Fix undefined behaviour in the lseek overflow check

rtems_filesystem_default_lseek_file() detected offset overflow by adding
the two offsets and then inspecting the wrapped-around result:

  off_t new_offset = reference_offset + offset;

  if (
    ( offset >= 0 && new_offset >= reference_offset ) ||
    ( offset < 0 && new_offset < reference_offset )
  ) {

By the time the comparison runs, signed integer overflow has already
occurred, which is undefined behaviour.  A compiler may assume that
"offset >= 0 && reference_offset + offset >= reference_offset" is always
true and fold the first arm of the test away.  The overflow branch then
becomes unreachable and the wrapped, negative sum falls into the
"new_offset >= 0" test instead, so lseek() reports EINVAL where POSIX
requires EOVERFLOW.

gcc happens to keep the wraparound and gets the right answer by luck;
clang folds the test and does not.  Nothing guarantees gcc will continue
to.

Use __builtin_add_overflow(), which both gcc and clang support and which
tests the condition without relying on undefined behaviour.

This fixes the fserror test on every filesystem that uses the default
lseek handler.  Before, on riscv/mbv built with clang:

  Testing lseek with arguments: "fd, huge, SEEK_END" EXPECT "EOVERFLOW"
  FAIL errno=Invalid argument  testsuites/fstests/fserror/test.c: 362

After, imfs_fserror, mimfs_fserror, mrfs_fserror, mdosfs_fserror,
fatfs_fserror, jffs2_fserror and jffs2nand_fserror all pass.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 cpukit/libfs/src/defaults/default_lseek_file.c | 15 ++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/cpukit/libfs/src/defaults/default_lseek_file.c b/cpukit/libfs/src/defaults/default_lseek_file.c
index 6f574333f1..208e9d3ab7 100644
--- a/cpukit/libfs/src/defaults/default_lseek_file.c
+++ b/cpukit/libfs/src/defaults/default_lseek_file.c
@@ -68,12 +68,17 @@ off_t rtems_filesystem_default_lseek_file(
   }
 
   if ( rv == 0 ) {
-    off_t new_offset = reference_offset + offset;
+    off_t new_offset;
 
-    if (
-      ( offset >= 0 && new_offset >= reference_offset ) ||
-      ( offset < 0 && new_offset < reference_offset )
-    ) {
+    /*
+     * Detecting the overflow by inspecting the wrapped-around sum is
+     * undefined behaviour, since signed integer overflow has already
+     * occurred by the time the comparison is made.  A compiler is entitled
+     * to assume "offset >= 0 && reference_offset + offset >= reference_offset"
+     * is always true and fold the test away, which yields EINVAL instead of
+     * EOVERFLOW.  Use the checked builtin instead.
+     */
+    if ( !__builtin_add_overflow( reference_offset, offset, &new_offset ) ) {
       if ( new_offset >= 0 ) {
         iop->offset = new_offset;
         rv = new_offset;
